  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1140 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nToday: Last Warm Day Before the Cool Down\n\nOne more mild, Fall day is in store for the Upper Midwest \nbefore cooler conditions begin to move in on Wednesday, but \nmoreso Thursday/Friday. High pressure remains in place over the \nregion for today leading to very light winds (5-10 mph or less) \nand dry conditions. Warm temperatures in the 50s are forecast \nonce again today with some locations reaching the 60 degree \nmark. The highest potential (30-60%) to reach 60 for a high will\nbe across southwestern Wisconsin.\n\nWednesday to Friday: Light Rain Chances and Cool Conditions\n\nThe stagnant zonal pattern becomes more progressive and active \non Wednesday as an upper level trough quickly digs south towards\nthe Midwest Wednesday into Thursday. Given the progressive \nnature of the trough, moisture quality will not be all that \ngreat out ahead of the system and will quickly decrease on the \nbackside of the trough Thursday morning. At the surface, a low \npressure system will trek east to southeast from the Dakotas to \nthe Mid Mississippi River Valley throughout the day. The upper \nlevel dynamics combined with the sufficient moisture should lead\nthe development of light rain showers across at least portions \nof the area on Wednesday. The chances that the rain accumulation\nis very light (0.10 inches or less) is still quite high with \nthose chances being 70 percent or higher according to the 10/01z\nNBM. The main push of cold air will lag behind the \nprecipitation bands so rain will be the precip type on \nWednesday. Plenty of dry air will be in place aloft by the time \nthe colder airmass arrives Wednesday night. Even so, atmospheric\nprofiles depict some saturation in the sfc-H800 layer Wednesday\nnight while the temperatures cool off considerably, so it \ncannot be ruled out that flurries or very light snow may fall \nduring the overnight hours. No accumulation would occur if any \nsnow is able to form. \n\nSpeaking of the cold air mass, temperatures on Wednesday will \ncool down, but should stay slightly above normal for one more \nday. Forecast highs should range from the mid 40s to lower 50s. \nThe cooler airmass arrives early Thursday morning with H850 \ntemperatures progged to be down to -5 to -8 Celsius by sunrise. \nMorning lows will be right around or below freezing area-wide to\nstart the day. The cool air will stick around through Friday \nand afternoon highs for both Thu/Fri will be in the lower 40s to\nupper 30s. Surprisingly enough, the NBM bias correction is not \nleading to much of an increase in temperatures on Thursday \ncompared to the raw NBM. However, that quickly changes on Friday\nwhere the bias correction is approximately 2 degrees on the \nwarmer side. Deterministic model output indicates temperatures \nmay stay in the 30s all day. Will keep the NBM highs for now \ngiven it is such a marginal change and it really does not change\nthe message that it will be the coldest airmass of the season \nthus far.\n\nNext Weekend: Slow Warming Trend with Dry Conditions\n\nSurface high pressure is expected to sink south into the Upper \nMidwest this upcoming weekend. This will keep conditions dry so \napart from the light precip chances on Wednesday, a dry forecast\nis on tap for the area through at least next Monday. \nTemperatures on Saturday remain cool in the 40s as the cooler \nairmass begins to exit off to the east. Highs warm back up to \naround normal on Sunday and that warming trend should continue \ninto the start of the next work week.",
